 13.\tab DECmate\par
\par
\tab The DECmate model series was released in the waning months of\par
\tab DEC\sub 12\nosupersub  canceling the OMNIBUS series (primarily PDP-8/A\sub 99\nosupersub  models).\par
\tab All DECmates have 32K of PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  memory (and 32K of Control Panel \tab memory, a feature unique to machines based on the 6100 or 6120\par
\tab series of one-chip CPU design).\par
\par
\tab The first model was released as DECmate; however, due to the \tab release of subsequent models, it is now referred to as DECmate I.\par
\par
\tab Unlike the DECmate I, the follow-on models (DECmate II, DECmate\par
\tab III and DECmate III+) require loadable firmware from the boot\par
\tab device to provide additional support; the firmware is common to \tab all three models.\par
\par
\tab While the typical configuration of the DECmate II includes a \tab single RX50\sub 99\nosupersub  dual-diskette drive, the system can be expanded to \tab include a second RX50\sub 99\nosupersub  dual-diskette drive.  Primarily for \tab backward compatibility, the RX278 option board can be added to \tab support up to two pairs of RX01/RX02 series 8" dual diskette\par
\tab drives.\par
\par
\tab Note: The DECmate II and DECmate III+ also support a hard disk \tab option known as the RD50\sub 99\nosupersub . (The RD50\sub 99\nosupersub  is included in the DECmate \tab III+.)  The DECmate II can support either the RX278\sub 99\nosupersub  option or \tab the RD50\sub 99\nosupersub  option (but not both simultaneously).\par
\par
\tab Option boards for the DECmate II series are incompatible with the \tab DECmate III series.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab The DECmate III models are housed in the smallest chassis ever \tab released by DEC\sub 12\nosupersub  for 12-bit computers; due to limited space, the \tab DECmate III+ has a single diskette drive and a half-height hard \tab disk drive.\line\line\tab Both the DECmate II and DECmate III series support a graphics\par
\tab option board and an option board with a Z80 processor chip\par
\tab capable of running CP/M-80.  An option board for the DECmate II\par
\tab was released which includes an 8086 processor chip as well as the\par
\tab Z80 processor.\page\cf1 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab Note: A similar board for the DECmate III series was considered, \tab but was withdrawn as production for DECmates was being\par
\tab discontinued.\par
\par
\tab A major software consideration of all DECmate models is that all \tab interfaces are based on the 6121 support chip; each 6121 chip\par
\tab supports five identical device interfaces.  However, all 6121\par
\tab interfaces are incompatible with the device 03/04 console \tab requirements of all 12-bit models from the PDP-5\sub 99\nosupersub  through the\par
\tab VT78\sub 99\nosupersub .  As such, only bastard subsets of OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  can be supported\par
\tab (with various system features missing).\line\line\tab Note: Due to superior design, P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  (as of Version 8V) can be\par
\tab run from any DECmate model once a suitable system device handler\par
\tab was made available.  The P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  system device handler for RX01\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab supports the DECmate I (and the DECmate II using the RX278 option\par
\tab board and an extended bootstrap).\par
\par
 \tab Time permitting (and access to a suitable hardware\par
\tab configuration), a functional RD50:\sub 99\nosupersub  hard disk system device \tab handler should be achievable.\par
\par
\tab Note: Due to interaction with Master Menu\sub 99\nosupersub , and other \tab considerations, additional utilities will be needed to prepare \tab the hard disk prior to system installation.  OS/278 Version 2\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab is not installed conventionally on these systems; instead an \tab image backup of a system is provided with a pragmatic restore \tab facility.  As such, the configuration is quite arbitrary; the\par
\tab installation of P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  demands greater flexibility for proper\par
\tab operation.\par
\par
 \tab The development of an RX50:\sub 99\nosupersub  system device handler has been\par
\tab deferred until P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  system generation techniques can support\par
\tab certain new features required specifically for this \tab configuration.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab The design size of the extended memory device handler extension\par
\tab (required by certain P?S/8\sub 99\cf1\nosupersub  \cf0 system device handlers) has evolved\par
\tab over the years to meet the challenges of various devices as \tab necessary.  The present length of eight additional pages of\par
\tab memory (and a two-page additional area to prepare the system for\par
\tab initial loading and related issues) was increased expressly for\par
\tab the RX50:\sub 99\nosupersub ; due to specific issues of the bootstap environment, \tab unique problems must be solved that are far more complicated than \tab any other configuration.\par
\par
\tab Note: OS/278 Version 2\sub 99\nosupersub  exhibits (documented) fatal flaws caused \tab by failure to overcome these system problems.  As such, execution\par
\tab of certain commands (that function normally on other\par
\tab configurations) causes permanent corruption of system \tab boot\par
\tab diskettes.  One of the goals of P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  RX50:\sub 99\nosupersub  support is the \tab elimination of this serious issue.\page\cf1 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
 14.\tab DECtape\par
\par
\tab DECtape is a removable media storage device based on the earlier\par
\tab LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  used on the classic \i LINC\sub\i0 99\nosupersub , LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub  and PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub  as an\par
\tab embedded subsystem of these machines.\cf1\par
\par
\tab\cf0 Note: While the media used for both DECtape and LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  is\par
\tab nominally the same magnetic material and tape width, the formats\par
\tab differ greatly.  The most obvious difference is that using\par
\tab DECtapes, the supply reel is mounted on the left side of the\par
\tab drive (while LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub  turn in the opposite direction).  Where\par
\tab applicable, plastic canisters for tape storage exist;  by\par
\tab convention, blue plastic canisters are used for DECtapes while\par
\tab green plastic canisters are used for LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub .  (This\par
\tab convention is often violated.)\par
\par
\tab DECtapes, much like LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , are individually block-\par
\tab replaceable and can be considered to be analogous to more typical\par
\tab rotating disk drives; however, block placement is for more\par
\tab critical due to the inherent overhead of locating a selected\par
\tab block by traversing all intervening blocks starting from the\par
\tab present tape position.  Additionally, there are considerations\par
\tab regarding the choice of initial search direction: Using the wrong\par
\tab initial  direction will cause undue delays because of the need to\par
\tab turn the tape around after a sufficient undershoot (which may be \tab avoidable).\cf1\par
\pard\qc\cf0\par
\pard\tab More successful operating systems use performance-enhancing\par
\tab techniques when accessing storage devices; lesser operating\par
\tab systems may take profoundly longer time to accomplish analogous\par
\tab tasks.\line\line\tab A case in point regarding DECtape is the ability to transfer\par
\tab multiple blocks in the same device handler call; once the current\par
\tab block has been transferred in the latest read or write operation, \tab starting a new device handler call must restart the overall\par
\tab operation for the latest desired block (because the motion bit\par
\tab must be cleared at the end of the latest transfer).  Assuming the\par
\tab next desired block is adjacent to the last block transferred\par
\tab previously, the delay in completing the next transfer will be\par
\tab palpable.  As such, more successful software strategies combine\par
\tab multiple block transfers in the same device handler operation to\par
\tab the extent possible.\par
\par
\tab LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub  can only transfer data moving in the forward\par
\tab direction.  Tapes in either format can search while moving in\par
\tab the reverse direction; however, DECtapes can also transfer data \tab once the desired block is located.  Certain software utilities\par
\tab take advantage of reverse motion data transfers, especially on\par
\tab certain DEC\sub 12\nosupersub  machines based on various word lengths.\page\cf1 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Unlike LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , the count of words in a DECtape block is\par
\tab subject to a restriction of being a whole multiple of 18-bit\par
\tab words.  As such, the standard format for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  is 129 12-\par
\tab bit words (or 86 18-bit words).\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab The underlying format of DECtape (first used on the PDP-1\sub 99\nosupersub  and\par
\tab PDP-4\sub 99\nosupersub ), was changed with the release of the type 552\sub 99\nosupersub  DECtape\par
\tab controller originally designed for the \i PDP-5\sub\i0 99\nosupersub .  As such, \tab DECtapes for the PDP-1\sub 99\nosupersub  and PDP-4\sub 99\nosupersub  are merely compatible with\par
\tab both systems and cannot share data with other computers that\par
\tab support DECtape.\par
\par
\tab Note: A tentative solution to this problem is available on the \tab LINC-8\sub 99.\cf1\nosupersub\par
\cf0\par
\tab For detailed information on other DEC\sub 12\nosupersub  machines, the user is\par
\tab directed to various documents on early computer architecture; the\par
 \tab PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  is the first successful DEC\sub 12\nosupersub  12-bit computer based on\par
\tab two's complement arithmetic (which is used throughout the\par
\tab computer industry on all subsequently created systems).\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab With the exception of very early Typeset-8\sub 99\nosupersub  systems, all PDP-8\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab models equipped with DECtape use a compatible DECtape controller\par
\tab and one or more compatible DECtape drives.\par
\par
\tab Controllers:\line\line\tab a) TC01\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\par
\tab b) TC08\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\par
\tab c) TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\par
\tab DECtape drives:\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab a) TU55\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\par
\tab b) TU56\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating system \i standard-bearer\i0  configuration is\par
\tab designed for use with DECtape as implemented on the TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  (or\par
\tab TC08\sub 99\nosupersub ) DECtape controller and TU55\sub 99\nosupersub  (or TU56\sub 99\nosupersub ) drives.\f1\fs22\par
